aScript's `replace()` is being interpreted as a special replacement pattern.\n\n\nIn JavaScript's `replace()`, the `$` character has special meaning, so `\\/` doesn't need escaping—the backslash itself is the escape. The real problem is that my function returns `'<\\/' + name` which becomes `<\\/CODE_QUOTE`, and that `\\/` is being interpreted as a special replacement pattern by the `replace()` method.\n\nLooking at the Go reference code more carefully, the replacement string is `'<\\/' + name`. In Go's `regexp` package, the replacement string can reference captured groups with `$1`, `$2`, etc., but `\\/` isn't a special sequence—it's literally a backslash followed by a forward slash. The key insight is that Go treats the backslash literally in this context, so the pattern `/ <\\/` in the replacement is meant to produce the literal `</` in the output.\n\nNow I'm examining the JavaScript regex pattern `/<\\/[\\t\\n\\r ]*(claim|code_quote|fixed_code|file_contents)/giu` to understand what it actually matches and how it behaves with the `u` flag for Unicode support.\n\nTesting this against `\"</CODE_QUOTE >\"` reveals a critical issue: the regex matches `</CODE_QUOTE` and the next character is a space (not a word character), so the space gets preserved in the output instead of being consumed. This means the replacement string `\"<\\/\" + name` produces `</CODE_QUOTE`, which is exactly what I want. Let me trace through the regex matching step by step to confirm the behavior.\n\nThe regex `/<\\/[\\t\\n\\r ]*(claim|code_quote|fixed_code|file_contents)/giu` starts by matching the literal `</`, then consumes any horizontal whitespace (zero or more spaces, tabs, newlines, or form feeds), then matches one of the four tags. When I trace `\"</CODE_QUOTE >\"` through it: `</` matches positions 0-1, the whitespace quantifier matches zero spaces since the next character is `C`, and then `(claim|code_quote|fixed_code|file_contents)` tries to match starting at position 2, which is `C